  tasks, so "caring" can't be about filtering [3mwhose[0m changes reach you. What's left is deciding [3mwhich of your own tasks [0m
  [3mare worth interrupting you for[0m, which is a real question but a different one. It's the difference between a
  subscription system and a reminder-rules system, and they have different data models.

  So, the last requirement question before I put approaches together:

  - [1mEvery dated task notifies[0m — a due date [3mis[0m the subscription. Zero extra UI, zero extra model. Cost: a big list means
    constant noise, and no way to quiet a task without deleting its date.
  - [1mExplicit opt-in per task[0m — a bell/"remind me" toggle. Closest to "tasks they care about" as written, and the intent
    is unambiguous. Cost: one more control per task, and users forget to set it, so notifications feel unreliable
    through no fault of the code.
  - [1mAttribute-driven[0m — starred or high-priority tasks notify automatically. No per-task chore, and interest rides on
    signals you'd want anyway. Cost: conflates "important" with "interrupt me," which aren't the same thing.
  - [1mUser-defined rules[0m — "1 day before anything tagged work." Most powerful and most future-proof; a rule engine absorbs
    sync and integration events later without redesign. Cost: by far the most machinery, and it's real UI work to make
    rules comprehensible.

  I'd recommend [1mexplicit opt-in per task, with a sensible default derived from the due date[0m — it matches your phrasing,
  the model stays small, and per-task reminder records generalize cleanly into rules later if you want them. The rule
  engine is the one I'd resist for v1: it's a lot of build for a single-user timer app, and YAGNI applies hard.
